<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fulda Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/744">744</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> Abbey was a Benedictine monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 744 by Sturm, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a>, who was buried there in 754. Under abbots such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a> it became one of the leading centres of learning in the Carolingian empire. Its abbots became princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and in 1752 the abbey was raised to a prince-bishopric.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fulda-abbey.jpg" alt="Fulda Abbey" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laurens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_St_George%27s_Cross.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-boniface">Saint Boniface</a> commissioned Sturm to build a larger church than any other Boniface had founded. In January 744 Sturm chose an unpopulated plot along the Fulda River, and the monastery's foundation dates to 12 March 744. Sturm visited monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, for models, and became the first abbot. In 751 Boniface had the house placed directly under the Holy See by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-zachary">Pope Zachary</a>, freeing it from the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. His tomb at Fulda made it a place of pilgrimage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The monks practised many trades, copied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and made goods that made the house wealthy, and some settled villages in the surrounding land. Under Abbot Baugulf the school had an inner school for Christian studies and an outer school for secular pupils, and in 787 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> praised it as a model. The monks' own account, the Supplex Libellus, tells how Abbot Ratgar's harsh rule and costly building led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> to exile him in 817. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a>, abbot from 822, gave the school separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> for the sciences, theology and the arts and gathered relics and manuscripts.</p><p>The library held about 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and preserved works such as the Annales of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> and the Res gestae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ammianus-marcellinus">Ammianus Marcellinus</a>. An Italian book-hunter probably found there in 1417 the last surviving manuscript of De rerum natura by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a>. The monks also kept the Annales necrologici, a list of the dead of the house for whom they prayed, which later took in patrons and nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the decline of Carolingian rule the abbey relied on other patrons. Its abbot was for several centuries primate of the Benedictine monasteries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and in 1221 Emperor Frederick II made the abbots princes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1356 Emperor Charles IV gave the prince-abbot the title of Archchancellor of the Empress. Nobles came to fill the abbey, and in the 14th century Count Johann of Ziegenhain led a rising against Prince-Abbot Heinrich VI. Much of the collection of Rabanus was lost when the Hessians looted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fulda">Fulda</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>.</p><p>The monastery was dissolved in 1802 and the principality secularised in 1803, though the diocese of Fulda continued. The territory passed through the Principality of Nassau-Orange-Fulda, French rule, the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, occupation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and in 1815 the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Princely Abbey of Fulda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The counts were advocates of <b>Fulda Abbey</b> and often in conflict with the landgraves of Hesse.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-ziegenhain">County of Ziegenhain</a></li></ul></section>
